# Linemen and Wide Receivers Reach Similar Player Load by Very Different Routes

**What it is** — Morris says that in his team's game data, offensive linemen and wide receivers have "very similar" total player load, but what makes up that load differs: the linemen have much lower explosive yardage, high-speed distance and max speed (QLdbKUiNadg [03:34], [04:04]). He defines player load as "a summation" of movement from the unit, "a really really really fancy Fitbit" (QLdbKUiNadg [03:34]). He compares the two to Bookers bourbon versus a lighter drink: both reach the same point, "it depends on how you get there" (caption spelling "Booker's"; the comparison is garbled in the captions, QLdbKUiNadg [05:06]). No numbers are read aloud, because the values were on slides the captions do not describe.

**Why it works** — The speaker gives no mechanism beyond the observation that linemen "bang all day" while receivers are running (QLdbKUiNadg [05:06]). Inference: player load adds up all movement, so a contact-heavy, short-burst profile and a running-heavy profile can sum to the same value.

**When to apply** — As a warning when one number is used across positions. Inference: a team-wide player-load target or a coach's comparison of "who worked harder" can mislead if the make-up is not checked. Eric's baseline: core GPS metrics tracked at Baylor include total distance, player load, velocity and acceleration bands (`baylor-applied-performance-playbook.md pp.11-13`), so position-level splits can be read next to player load.

**When NOT to apply** — Confidence is untested: the figures are not stated in the audio, so the size of "very similar" and "very different" cannot be checked. One team, one season, two position groups. Do not read it as showing that player load is a poor metric, which the speaker does not claim.

**Example** — The talk's own case: a line coach threw the first report in the trash, and Morris reframed the data around what the load consisted of for linemen (QLdbKUiNadg [04:04], [04:34]).

**Related** — [[framework--slice-plays-and-count-contacts-to-define-lineman-game-demand]], [[heuristic--show-coaches-and-athletes-the-data-in-their-terms]].
